```ruby
class Proxy
  def initialize(target)
    @target = target
  end

  def method_missing(name, *args, &block)
    if @target.respond_to?(name)
      @target.send(name, *args, &block)
    else
      super
    end
  end

  def respond_to_missing?(name, include_private = false)
    @target.respond_to?(name, include_private) || super
  end
end
```

**Explanation:**

- `initialize` simply stores the wrapped object.
- `method_missing` is triggered whenever a method not directly defined on `Proxy` is called. It checks whether `@target` responds to that method; if so, it forwards the call (with all arguments and an optional block) via `send`. Otherwise it calls `super`, which raises the standard `NoMethodError`.
- `respond_to_missing?` makes `respond_to?` report `true` for any method the target supports (in addition to anything `Proxy` itself defines), and falls back to `super` otherwise.
- Ruby's built-in `Object#method` uses `respond_to_missing?` to decide whether a method exists. If it returns `true`, `method(:name)` will succeed and produce a `Method` object that, when called, dispatches through `method_missing` — exactly reproducing the same forwarding behavor as calling the method directly on the proxy. If `respond_to_missing?` returns `false`, `method(:name)` naturally raises `NameError`, matching the required behavior.

