class Ledger
  def initialize(entries = [])
    @entries = entries.dup
  end

  def add(entry)
    @entries << entry
    self
  end

  def entries
    @entries
  end

  def freeze
    @entries.freeze
    super
  end

  def initialize_dup(other)
    super
    @entries = other.entries.dup
  end

  def initialize_clone(other)
    super
    @entries = other.entries.dup
    @entries.freeze if other.frozen?
  end
end
```

This implementation:

- Stores entries in an internal array, always duplicating the input array on construction so the ledger doesn't share state with the caller's array.
- `#add` appends and returns `self`.
- `#freeze` freezes the internal entries array as well as the ledger itself (via `super`), ensuring both `#add` and direct mutation of the array returned by `#entries` raise `FrozenError` afterward.
- `#dup` uses `initialize_dup` to give the copy its own independent (unfrozen) entries array; since `dup` never carries over frozen status, the copy is always unfrozen and can be added to without affecting the original.
- `#clone` uses `initialize_clone` to give the copy its own entries array, freezing that array too if the original was frozen. Ruby's built-in `clone` behavior automatically preserves the frozen state of the object itself, so the copy stays frozen exactly when the original was.

